WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.360
When you're building reliable agent systems, tool selection is one of the first things that breaks

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:11.359
in subtle ways. You might expect the tool name to be the key signal, but it's not. The description

3
00:00:11.359 --> 00:00:16.959
is. Here's why that matters for the Architect Foundations exam and for building systems that

4
00:00:16.959 --> 00:00:22.959
route correctly under real-world conditions. Every tool choice Claude makes starts with a

5
00:00:22.959 --> 00:00:29.440
block of natural language text you wrote. A tool description is the string in a tool definition

6
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:35.759
that tells Claude what the tool does, when to use it, and what it returns. And Claude treats that

7
00:00:35.759 --> 00:00:42.159
text as the primary signal for choosing a tool. That matching process, Claude aligning a user's

8
00:00:42.159 --> 00:00:48.319
request to the right tool, is the whole game. When that match fails, Claude calls the wrong tool,

9
00:00:48.319 --> 00:00:53.919
and that usually traces back to a description that was vague, incomplete, or missing the context

10
00:00:53.919 --> 00:01:00.000
Claude needed. Those ideas frame everything else in this lesson, because every fix we discuss

11
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.839
comes back to the description. Here's how tool selection actually plays out. When a request

12
00:01:05.839 --> 00:01:11.680
comes in, Claude reads every tool description in the current context. It's not just skimming for

13
00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:18.000
keywords, it's doing semantic matching, comparing the intent of the request against the full meaning

14
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.839
of each description. The tool name is a hint. It might reinforce the match, but it doesn't determine

15
00:01:23.839 --> 00:01:30.239
it. What determines it is how precisely the description captures what the tool does and when

16
00:01:30.239 --> 00:01:36.720
to reach for it. A strong description makes one tool the obvious winner. A weak description makes

17
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:43.680
every tool equally plausible, which is exactly the problem we're trying to avoid. It's worth putting

18
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:50.000
strong and weak descriptions side by side. A strong description tells Claude what the tool does,

19
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:56.720
what inputs it expects, what it returns, and when to call it. Think of retrieves customer order

20
00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:03.919
history by customer ID and date range, returning a list of order objects with status and timestamps

21
00:02:03.919 --> 00:02:11.679
versus handles orders. Weak descriptions fail in a few recognizable ways. Vague intent says what a

22
00:02:11.679 --> 00:02:17.520
tool is but not when to use it, so Claude can't separate it from alternatives with similar scope.

23
00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.440
Missing scope leaves out what the tool doesn't handle, so Claude has no boundary to route around.

24
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.199
No output signal leaves Claude unable to confirm that calling the tool produces what the request

25
00:02:29.199 --> 00:02:35.360
needs. Let's walk through how Claude actually evaluates its options. It reads all available

26
00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:41.199
descriptions in context and assigns implicit relevance weights based on how well each one

27
00:02:41.199 --> 00:02:47.199
matches the current request semantically. The tool with the highest confidence match gets selected.

28
00:02:47.759 --> 00:02:53.600
No single keyword flips the decision. It's the full descriptive context that matters. This is

29
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:59.039
important because it means you can't game the system by stuffing keywords into a short description.

30
00:02:59.039 --> 00:03:04.479
You need a complete coherent description that genuinely represents what the tool does and when

31
00:03:04.479 --> 00:03:10.240
to use it. The evaluation happens every time, so every request goes through the same semantic

32
00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:16.240
matching process regardless of which tool was called previously. There are four selection

33
00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:22.160
outcomes you should be able to name. A correct call means the right tool was selected immediately

34
00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.960
because the description was precise. A miss route means the wrong tool was called,

35
00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.960
usually because descriptions overlapped or lacked specificity. A no call means no tool

36
00:03:33.039 --> 00:03:37.279
matched the request, meaning the description failed to capture the intent at all.

37
00:03:37.839 --> 00:03:43.279
And a fallback means Claude couldn't select confidently and ask the user to clarify

38
00:03:43.279 --> 00:03:48.800
rather than guessing. Each of these outcomes maps directly back to description quality.

39
00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:54.800
That's the lever you control as the developer. You can't change how Claude does semantic matching,

40
00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.960
but you can write descriptions that make the right answer obvious every time.

41
00:03:59.919 --> 00:04:05.839
A common developer assumption is that naming tools clearly is enough. It isn't. Think about

42
00:04:05.839 --> 00:04:12.479
a tool named search. That name could refer to a web search, a database query, a file system search,

43
00:04:12.479 --> 00:04:18.640
or a vector store lookup. Claude doesn't infer purpose from naming conventions alone. Tools

44
00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:24.399
named similarly but serving different purposes will collide, and Claude will miss route unless

45
00:04:24.559 --> 00:04:30.079
the descriptions clearly separate them. The name gets you a starting point for human readers.

46
00:04:30.079 --> 00:04:35.679
The description is what actually tells Claude which tool to reach for and when. That's the

47
00:04:35.679 --> 00:04:40.959
distinction the exam tests, and it's the one most developers miss until they've seen their

48
00:04:40.959 --> 00:04:46.799
first production miss routing failure. The Architect Foundations exam has a specific

49
00:04:46.799 --> 00:04:53.040
trap on this topic, and you need to know it cold. Distractor answers will tell you that tool names

50
00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:59.440
are what drives tool selection. They're wrong. Other distractors might point to input schemas,

51
00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:05.359
output types, or function signatures as the primary driver of selection. Those all play

52
00:05:05.359 --> 00:05:10.079
a role in good tool design, but none of them is the primary signal Claude selects on.

53
00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:15.920
The description is. Even a perfectly named tool with a clean, well-structured schema

54
00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:21.440
will miss route if the description is vague or incomplete. That's the key insight the exam is

55
00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.720
testing, and it's the insight that separates engineers who understand agent architecture

56
00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:33.519
from those who are guessing. Think of every tool description as a small routing contract you sign

57
00:05:33.519 --> 00:05:39.679
with Claude. It reads all of them together, weighs them against the requests, and calls whichever one

58
00:05:39.679 --> 00:05:46.799
reads the closest. That's the whole mechanism. The contract only holds if it says when to reach for

59
00:05:46.799 --> 00:05:52.160
the tool and what comes back, because a name on its own leaves Claude trying to guess.

60
00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:56.000
That closes out this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

